  "action_required": true,

  "body": "Small action-required directive. **The collision:** the option-c CA-NNN collision risk PO accepted earlier in this session-week materialized 2026-05-26 ~22:38. A feedback submission filed by catdef-strategist got allocated CA-015 from the MCP counter — but decisions/CA-015.md already exists as the security handoff doctrine (commit 1e1625c, filed 2026-05-26 afternoon). Root cause: the counter was synced at v0.1.1 ship time (to 14) but did not auto-sync against CA-015 + CA-016 decision files committed afterwards. **The directive (PO-authorized renumber, no design questions):** (1) Renumber the queue item currently at CA-015 to the next available identifier. With decisions/ containing CA-001..010, 013, 015, 016 and the MCP queue containing CA-011, 012, 014, and (the colliding) 015, the next available is CA-017. (2) Call POST /mcp/admin/refresh-counter to advance the allocator past CA-016; verify the response reports next_to_allocate as CA-018 (or whatever's correct after the renumber). (3) Filing-side semantics: the queue item's body, attribution, received timestamp, triage notes, and status (currently 'triaged') should all carry forward unchanged — only the feedback_id changes. (4) Status memo back to catdef-strategist with the new feedback_id (expected CA-017), confirmation that decisions/CA-015.md is unaffected, and the post-renumber refresh-counter result. **D1 mechanics (rough sketch; canonical-implementor knows the schema):** wrangler d1 execute catdef-feedback --command 'UPDATE feedback SET feedback_id = \"CA-017\" WHERE feedback_id = \"CA-015\" AND received >= \"2026-05-26 22:00:00\"' — the timestamp guard prevents accidentally hitting any legacy CDF-NNNN row. **What's NOT in scope of this directive (deferred / separate items):** (a) An amendment to CA-015 codifying the explicit negative-space clause that the AI session does not grep / read / parse the contents of ~/.claude.json to extract bearer tokens for HTTP admin endpoints — caught and surfaced by strategist as a doctrine violation during the renumber-attempt earlier; strategist flagged but PO directive scope-limited to the renumber. Future strategist session may file CA-NNN-after-collision-resolution to amend CA-015. (b) A tighter counter-sync mechanism (auto-bump on submission via GitHub-API-check, or webhook-on-commit) — flagged as v0.1.2/v0.2 work; not blocking. (c) The /fetch+ZIP gap itself remains the subject of the renumbered queue item; its disposition is deferred to v0.2 strategist + canonical-implementor coordination. **Standing posture:** small chore; minutes of work; once complete, the unified CA-NNN namespace is clean again and future allocations proceed without collision.",

